"[2602.14211v1] SkillJect: Automating Stealthy Skill-Based Prompt Injection for Coding Agents with Trace-Driven Closed-Loop Refinement (https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.14211v1)\nciteturn31838view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.14211v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 163\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Cryptography and Security\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 15 Feb 2026 (this version), latest version 16 Jun 2026 (cite5†v3 )]\nL9: # Title:SkillJect: Automating Stealthy Skill-Based Prompt Injection for Coding Agents with Trace-Driven Closed-Loop Refinement\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Xiaojun Jia , cite7†Jie Liao , cite8†Simeng Qin , cite9†Jindong Gu , cite10†Wenqi Ren , cite11†Xiaochun Cao , cite12†Yang Liu , cite13†Philip Torr L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled SkillJect: Automating Stealthy Skill-Based Prompt Injection for Coding Agents with Trace-Driven Closed-Loop Refinement, by Xiaojun Jia and 7 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite14†View PDF cite15†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Agent skills are becoming a core abstraction in coding agents, packaging long-form instructions and auxiliary scripts to extend tool-augmented behaviors. This abstraction introduces an under-measured attack surface: skill-based prompt injection, where poisoned skills can steer agents away from user intent and safety policies.\nL17: In practice, naive injections often fail because the malicious intent is too explicit or drifts too far from the original skill, leading agents to ignore or refuse them; existing attacks are also largely hand-crafted. We propose the first automated framework for stealthy prompt injection tailored to agent skills.\nL18: The framework forms a closed loop with three agents: an Attack Agent that synthesizes injection skills under explicit stealth constraints, a Code Agent that executes tasks using the injected skills in a realistic tool environment, and an Evaluate Agent that logs action traces (e.g., tool calls and file operations) and verifies whether targeted malicious behaviors occurred.\nL19: We also propose a malicious payload hiding strategy that conceals adversarial operations in auxiliary scripts while injecting optimized inducement prompts to trigger tool execution. Extensive experiments across diverse coding-agent settings and real-world software engineering tasks show that our method consistently achieves high attack success rates under realistic settings.\nL20: Subjects:  | Cryptography and Security (cs.CR); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite16†arXiv:2602.14211 [cs.CR]\nL22:    | (or cite17†arXiv:2602.14211v1 [cs.CR] for this version)\nL23:    | cite18†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2602.14211†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Xiaojun Jia [cite19†view email ]\nL27: [v1] Sun, 15 Feb 2026 16:09:48 UTC (490 KB)\nL28: cite20†[v2] Sat, 16 May 2026 20:34:44 UTC (593 KB)\nL29: cite5†[v3] Tue, 16 Jun 2026 17:33:00 UTC (605 KB)\nL30: \nL31: Full-text links:\nL32: \nL33: ## Access Paper:\nL34: \nL35: View a PDF of the paper titled SkillJect: Automating Stealthy Skill-Based Prompt Injection for Coding Agents with Trace-Driven Closed-Loop Refinement, by Xiaojun Jia and 7 other authors\nL36: \nL37:   * cite14†View PDF L38:   * cite15†HTML (experimental) L39:   * cite21†TeX Source L40: \nL41: cite22†view license L42: ### Current browse context:\nL43: \nL44: cs.CR\nL45: \nL46: cite23†< prev |   cite24†next > L47: \nL48: cite25†new | cite26†recent | cite27†2026-02 L49: \nL50: Change to browse by:\nL51: \nL52: cite28†cs L53: cite29†cs.AI L54: \nL55: ### Re